\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.29 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja then said to Lord V\'e4manadeva: O br\'e4hma\'eba, I offer You my hearty welcome and my respectful obeisances. Please let us know what we may do for You. We think of You as the perso
nified austerity of the great br\'e4hma\'eba-sages.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.30 - O my Lord, because You have kindly arrived at our home, all my forefathers are satisfied, our family and entire dynasty have been sanctified, and the sacrifice we are performing is now complete be
cause of Your presence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.31 - O son of a br\'e4hma\'eba, today the fire of sacrifice is ablaze according to the injunction of the \'e7\'e4
stra, and I have been freed from all the sinful reactions of my life by the water that has washed Your lotus feet. O my Lord, by the touch of Your small lotus feet the entire surface of the world has been sanctified.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.32 - O son of a br\'e4hma\'eba, it appears that You have come here to ask me for something. Therefore, whatever You want You may take from me. O best of those
 who are worshipable. You may take from me a cow, gold, a furnished house, palatable food and drink, the daughter of a br\'e4hma\'eba for Your wife, prosperous villages, horses, elephants, chariots or whatever You desire.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  }{\f35\fs28\cf2\insrsid4605116 Lord V\'e4manadeva, the Dwarf Incarnation
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat8 {\fs28\cf2\insrsid4605116   \bullet   }{
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid4605116\charrsid4605116 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10883103\charrsid4605116 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.1 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: After Lord Brahm\'e4
 had thus spoken, glorifying the Supreme Lord's activities and prowess, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is never subject to death like an ordinary living being, appeared from the womb of Aditi. His four hands were decorated with a co
nchshell, club, lotus and disc, He was dressed in yellow garments, and His eyes appeared like the petals of a blooming lotus.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.2 - The body of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, blackish in complexion, was free from all inebrieties. His lotus face
, decorated with earrings resembling sharks, appeared very beautiful, and on His bosom was the mark of \'c7r\'e9
vatsa. He wore bangles on His wrists, armlets on His arms, a helmet on His head, a belt on His waist, a sacred thread across His chest, and ankle bells decorating His lotus feet.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.3 - An uncommonly beautiful garland of flowers decorated His bosom, and because the flowers were extremely fragrant, a large group of bees, making their natural humming sounds, invaded them for honey. When the Lord app
eared, wearing the Kaustubha gem on His neck, His effulgence vanquished the darkness in the home of the Praj\'e4pati Ka\'e7yapa.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.4 - At that time, there was happiness in all directions, in the reservoirs of water like the rivers and oceans, and in the c
ore of everyone's heart. The various seasons displayed their respective qualities, and all living entities in the upper planetary system, in outer space and on the surface of the earth were jubilant. The demigods, the cows, the br\'e4hma\'eb
as and the hills and mountains were all filled with joy.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.5 - On the day of \'c7rava\'eba-dv\'e4da\'e7\'e9 [the twelfth day of the bright fortnight in the month of Bh\'e4dra], when the moon came into the lunar mansion \'c7rava\'eb
a, at the auspicious moment of Abhijit, the Lord appeared in this universe. Considering the Lord's appearance very auspicious, all the stars and planets, from the sun to Saturn, were munificently charitable.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.6 - O King, when the Lord appeared-on dv\'e4da\'e7\'e9, the twelfth day of the moon-the sun was at the meridian, as every learned scholar knows. This dv\'e4da\'e7\'e9 is called Vijay\'e4.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.7 - Conchshells, kettledrums, drums, pa\'ebavas and \'e4nakas vibrated in concert. The sound of these and various other instruments was tumultuous.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.8 - Being very pleased, the celestial dancing girls [Apsar\'e4s] danced in jubilation, the best of the Gandharvas sang songs, and the great sages, demigods, Manus, Pit\'e4s and fire-gods offered prayers to satisfy the Lord.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.9-10 - The Siddhas, Vidy\'e4dharas, Kimpuru\'f1as, Kinnaras, C\'e4ra\'ebas, Yak\'f1as, R\'e4k\'f1asas, Supar\'eb
as, the best of serpents, and the followers of the demigods all showered flowers on Aditi's residence, covering the entire house, while glorifying and praising the Lord and dancing.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.11 - When Aditi saw the Supreme Perso
nality of Godhead, who had appeared from her own womb, having accepted a transcendental body by His own spiritual potency, she was struck with wonder and was very happy. Upon seeing the child, Praj\'e4pati Ka\'e7
yapa exclaimed, "Jaya! Jaya!" in great happiness and wonder.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 
SB 8.18.12 - The Lord appeared in His original form, with ornaments and weapons in His hands. Although this ever-existing form is not visible in the material world, He nonetheless appeared in this form. Then, in the presence of His father and m
other, He assumed the form of V\'e4mana, a br\'e4hma\'eba-dwarf, a brahmac\'e4r\'e9, just like a theatrical actor.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.13 - When the great sages saw the Lord as the brahmac\'e4r\'e9-dwarf V\'e4mana, they were certainly very pleased. Thus they placed before them Ka\'e7yapa Muni, the Praj\'e4
pati, and performed all the ritualistic ceremonies, such as the birthday ceremony.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.14 - At the sacred thread ceremony of V\'e4manadeva, the sun-god personally uttered the G\'e4yatr\'e9 mantra, B\'e5haspati offered the sacred thread, and Ka\'e7yapa Muni offered a straw belt.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.15 - Mother earth gave Him a deerskin, and the demigod of the moon, who is the king of the forest, gave Him a brahma-da\'eb\'f2a [the rod of a brahmac\'e4r\'e9
]. His mother, Aditi, gave Him cloth for underwear, and the deity presiding over the heavenly kingdom offered Him an umbrella.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.16 - O King, Lord Brahm\'e4 offered a waterpot to the inexhaustible Supreme Personality of Godhead, the seven sages offered Him ku\'e7a grass, and mother Sarasvat\'e9 gave Him a string of Rudr\'e4k\'f1a beads.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.17 - When V\'e4manadeva had thus been given the sacred thread, Kuvera, King of the Yak\'f1as, gave Him a pot for begging alms, and mother Bhagavat\'e9, the wife of Lord \'c7
iva and most chaste mother of the entire universe, gave Him His first alms.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.18 - Having thus been welcomed by everyone, Lord V\'e4manadeva, the best of the brahmac\'e4r\'e9s, exhibited His Brahman effulgence. Thus He surpassed in beauty that entire assembly, which was filled with great saintly br
\'e4hma\'ebas.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.19 - After Lord \'c7r\'e9 V\'e4manadeva set a sacrificial fire, He offered worship and performed a fire sacrifice on the sacrificial field.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.20 - When the Lord heard that Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was performing a\'e7vamedha sacrifices under the patronage of br\'e4hma\'ebas belonging to the Bh\'e5gu dynasty, 
the Supreme Lord, who is full in every respect, proceeded there to show His mercy to Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja. By His weight, He pushed down the earth with every step.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.21 - While engaged in performing the sacrifice in the field known as Bh\'e5gukaccha, on the northern bank of the Narmad\'e4 River, the brahminical priests, the descendants of Bh\'e5gu, saw V\'e4
manadeva to be like the sun rising nearby.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.22 - O King, because of V\'e4manadeva's bright effulgence, the priests, along with Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja and all the member
s of the assembly, were robbed of their splendor. Thus they began to ask one another whether the sun-god himself, Sanat-kum\'e4ra or the fire-god had personally come to see the sacrificial ceremony.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.23 - While the priests of the Bh\'e5gu dynasty and their disciples talked and argued in various ways, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, V\'e4
manadeva, holding in His hands the rod, the umbrella and a waterpot full of water, entered the arena of the a\'e7vamedha sacrifice.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.24-25 - Appearing as a br\'e4hma\'eba boy, wearing a belt of straw, a sacred thread, an upper garment of deerskin, and matted locks of hair, Lord V\'e4
manadeva entered the arena of sacrifice. His brilliant effulgence diminished the brilliance of all the priests and their disciples, who thus stood from their seats and welcomed the Lord properly by offering obeisances.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.26 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, jubilant at seeing Lord V\'e4manadeva, whose beautiful limbs contributed equally to the beauty of His entire body, offered Him a seat with great satisfaction.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.27 - Thus offering a proper reception to the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is always beautiful to the liberated souls, Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja worshiped Him by washing His lotus feet.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.28 - Lord \'c7iva, the best of demigods, who carries on his forehead the emblem of the moon, receives on his head with great devotion the Ganges water emanating from the toe of Vi\'f1\'eb
u. Being aware of religious principles, Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja knew this. Consequently, following in the footsteps of Lord \'c7iva, he also placed on his head the water that had washed the Lord's lotus feet.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.29 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja then said to Lord V\'e4manadeva: O br\'e4hma\'eba, I offer You my hearty welcome and my respectful obeisances. Please let us know what we may do for You. We think of You as th
e personified austerity of the great br\'e4hma\'eba-sages.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.30 - O my Lord, because You have kindly arrived at our home, all my forefathers are satisfied, our family and entire dynasty have been sanctified, and the sacrifice we are performing is now comp
lete because of Your presence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.31 - O son of a br\'e4hma\'eba, today the fire of sacrifice is ablaze according to the injunction of the \'e7\'e4
stra, and I have been freed from all the sinful reactions of my life by the water that has washed Your lotus feet. O my Lord, by the touch of Your small lotus feet the entire surface of the world has been sanctified.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.18.32 - O son of a br\'e4hma\'eba, it appears that You have come here to ask me for something. Therefore, whatever You want You may take from me. O best o
f those who are worshipable. You may take from me a cow, gold, a furnished house, palatable food and drink, the daughter of a br\'e4hma\'eba for Your wife, prosperous villages, horses, elephants, chariots or whatever You desire.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Translations 1-32
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid4605116\charrsid4605116 \chftn 
{\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10883103\charrsid4605116 \chftn }{
\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.1 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When the Supreme Personality of Godhead, V\'e4manadeva, heard Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja speaking in this pleasing way, He was very satisfied, for Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja had spoken in terms of religious principles. Thus the Lord began to praise him.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.2 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead said: O King, you are indeed exalted because your present advisors are the br\'e4hma\'ebas who are descendants of Bh\'e5
gu and because your instructor for your future life is your grandfather, the peaceful and venerable Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja.
 Your statements are very true, and they completely agree with religious etiquette. They are in keeping with the behavior of your family, and they enhance your reputation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.3 - I know that even until now, no one taking birth in your family has been poor-minded or miserly. No one has refused to give charity to br\'e4hma\'eb
as, nor after promising to give charity has anyone failed to fulfill his promise.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.4 - O King Bali, never in your dynasty has the low-minded King been born who upon being requested has refused charity to br\'e4hma\'ebas in holy places or a fight to k\'f1
atriyas on a battlefield. And your dynasty is even more glorious due to the presence of Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, who is like the beautiful moon in the sky.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.5 - It was in your dynasty that Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a was born. Carrying only his own club, he wandered the globe alone, without assistance, to conquer all directions, and no hero he met could rival him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.6 - When delivering the earth from the Garbhodaka Sea, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, in His incarnation as a boar, killed Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a, who had appeared before Him. The fight was severe, and the Lord killed Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1
a with great difficulty. Later, as the Lord thought about the uncommon prowess of Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a, He felt Himself victorious indeed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.7 - When Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu heard the news of his brother's being killed, with great anger he went to the residence of Vi\'f1\'ebu, the killer of his brother, wanting to kill Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.8 - Seeing Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu coming forward bearing a trident in his hand like personified death, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the best of all mystics and the knower of the progress of time, thought as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.9 - Wheresoever I go, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu will follow Me, as death follows all living entities. Therefore it is better for Me to enter the c
ore of his heart, for then, because of his power to see only externally, he will not see Me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.10 - Lord V\'e4manadeva continued: O King of the demons, after Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu made this decision, He entered the body of His enemy Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, who was running
 after Him with great force. In a subtle body inconceivable to Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, who was in great anxiety, entered Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's nostril along with his breath.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.11 - Upon seeing that the residence of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu was vacant, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu began searching for Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu everywhere. Angry at not seeing Him, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu screamed loudly and searched the entire universe, including the surface of the earth, the higher planetary systems, all directions and all the caves and oceans. But Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, the greatest hero, did not see Vi\'f1\'ebu anywhere.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.12 - Unable to see Him, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu said, "I have searched the entire universe, but I could not find Vi\'f1\'ebu, who has killed my brother. Therefore, He must certainly have gone to that place from
 which no one returns. [In other words, He must now be dead.]"
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.13 - Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's anger against Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu persisted until his death. Other people in the bodily concept of life maintain anger only because of false ego and the great influence of ignorance.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.14 - Your father, Virocana, the son of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Prahl\'e4da, was very affectionate toward br\'e4hma\'ebas. Although he knew very well that it was the demigods who had come to him in the dress of br\'e4hma\'eb
as, at their request he delivered to them the duration of his life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.15 - You also have observed the principles followed by great personalities who are householder br\'e4hma\'ebas, by your forefathers and by great heroes who are extremely famous for their exalted activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.16 - O King of the Daityas, from Your Majesty, who come from such a noble family and who are able to give charity munificently, I ask only three paces of land, to the measurement of My steps.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.17 - O King, controller of the entire universe, although you are very munificent and are able to give Me as much land as I want, I do not want anything from you that is unnecessary. If a learned br\'e4hma\'eb
a takes charity from others only according to his needs, he does not become entangled in sinful activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.18 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja said: O son of a br\'e4hma\'eb
a, Your instructions are as good as those of learned and elderly persons. Nonetheless, You are a boy, and Your intelligence is insufficient. Thus You are not very prudent in regard to Your self-interest.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.19 -
 I am able to give You an entire island because I am the proprietor of the three divisions of the universe. You have come to take something from me and have pleased me by Your sweet words, but You are asking only three paces of land. Therefore You are not
 very intelligent.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.20 - O small boy, one who approaches me to beg something should not have to ask anything more, anywhere. Therefore, if You wish, You may ask from me as much land as will suffice to maintain You according to Your needs.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.21 - The Personality of Godhead said: O my dear King, even the entirety of whatever there may be within the three worlds to satisfy one's senses cannot satisfy a person whose senses are uncontrolled.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.22 - If I were not satisfied with three paces of land, then surely I would not be satisfied even with possessing one of the seven islands, consisting of nine var\'f1
as. Even if I possessed one island, I would hope to get others.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.23 - We have heard that although powerful kings like Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5thu and Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Gaya achieved proprietorship over the seven dv\'e9pas, they could not achieve satisfaction or find the end of their ambitions.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.24 - One should be satisfied with whatever he achieves by his previous destiny, for discontent can never bring happiness. A person who is not self-controlled will not be happy even with possessing the three worlds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.25 - Material existence causes discontent in regard to fulfilling one's lusty desires and achieving more and more money. This is the cause for 
the continuation of material life, which is full of repeated birth and death. But one who is satisfied by that which is obtained by destiny is fit for liberation from this material existence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.26 - A br\'e4hma\'eba who is satisfied with whatever is providentially obtained is increasingly enlightened with spiritual power, but the spiritual potency of a dissatisfied br\'e4hma\'eb
a decreases, as fire diminishes in potency when water is sprinkled upon it.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.27 - Therefore, O King, from you, the best of those
 who give charity, I ask only three paces of land. By such a gift I shall be very pleased, for the way of happiness is to be fully satisfied to receive that which is absolutely needed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.28 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When the Supreme Personality of Godhead had thus spoken to Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, Bali smiled and told Him, "All right. Take whatever You like." To confirm his promise to give V\'e4
manadeva the desired land, he then took up his waterpot.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.29 - Understanding Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu's purpose, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, the best of the learned, immediately spoke as follows to his disciple, who was about to offer everything to Lord V\'e4manadeva.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.30 - \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya said: O son of Virocana, this brahmac\'e4r\'e9 in the form of a dwarf is directly the imperishable Supreme Personality of Godhead, Vi\'f1\'ebu. Accepting Ka\'e7
yapa Muni as His father and Aditi as His mother, He has now appeared in order to fulfill the interests of the demigods.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.31 - You do not know what a dangerous position you have accepted by promising to give Him land. I do not think that this promise is good for you. It will bring great harm to the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.32 - This person falsely appearing as a brahmac\'e4r\'e9 is actually the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, who has come in this form to take away a
ll your land, wealth, beauty, power, fame and education. After taking everything from you, He will deliver it to Indra, your enemy.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.33 - You have promised to give Him three steps of land in charity, but when you give it He will occupy the three wo
rlds. You are a rascal! You do not know what a great mistake you have made. After giving everything to Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, you will have no means of livelihood. How then shall you live?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.34 - V\'e4manadeva will first occupy the three worlds with one step, the
n He will take His second step and occupy everything in outer space, and then He will expand His universal body to occupy everything. Where will you offer Him the third step?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.35 - You will certainly be unable to fulfill your promise, and I think that because of this inability your eternal residence will be in hell.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.36 - Learned scholars do not praise that charity which endangers one's own livelihood. Charity, sacrifice, austerity and fruitive activities are possible for one who is competen
t to earn his livelihood properly. [They are not possible for one who cannot maintain himself.]
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.37 - Therefore one who is in full knowledge should divide his accumulated wealth in five parts-for religion, for reputation, for opulence, for sense gr
atification and for the maintenance of his family members. Such a person is happy in this world and in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.38 - One might argue that since you have already promised, how can you refuse? O best of the demons, just take from me the evidence of the Bahv\'e5ca-\'e7ruti, which says that a promise is truthful preceded by the word o
\'e0 and untruthful if not.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.39 - The Vedas enjoin that the factual result of the tree of the body is the good fruits and flowers derived from it. But if the bodily tree d
oes not exist, there is no possibility of factual fruits and flowers. Even if the body is based on untruth, there cannot be factual fruits and flowers without the help of the bodily tree.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.40 - When a tree is uprooted it immediately falls down and 
begins to dry up. Similarly, if one doesn't take care of the body, which is supposed to be untruth-in other words, if the untruth is uprooted-the body undoubtedly becomes dry.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.41 - The utterance of the word o\'e0 signifies separation from one's monet
ary assets. In other words, by uttering this word one becomes free from attachment to money because his money is taken away from him. To be without money is not very satisfactory, for in that position one cannot fulfill one's desires. In other words, by u
sing the word o\'e0 one becomes poverty-stricken. Especially when one gives charity to a poor man or beggar, one remains unfulfilled in self-realization and in sense gratification.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.42 - Therefore, the safe course is to say no. Although it is a falseho
od, it protects one completely, it draws the compassion of others toward oneself, and it gives one full facility to collect money from others for oneself. Nonetheless, if one always pleads that he has nothing, he is condemned, for he is a dead body while 
living, or while still breathing he should be killed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.43 - In flattering a woman to bring her under control, in joking, in a marriage ceremony, in earning one's livelihood, when one's life is in danger, in protecting cows and brahminical culture, o
r in protecting a person from an enemy's hand, falsity is never condemned.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  19. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid4605116\charrsid4605116 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10883103\charrsid4605116 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.1 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When the Supreme Personality of Godhead, V\'e4
manadeva, heard Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja speaking in this pleasing way, He was very satisfied, for Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja had spoken in terms of religious principles. Thus the Lord began to praise him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.2 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead said: O King, you are indeed exalted because your present advisors are the br\'e4hma\'ebas who are descendants of Bh\'e5gu and bec
ause your instructor for your future life is your grandfather, the peaceful and venerable Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja. Your statements are very true, and they completely agree with religious etiquette. They are in keeping with the behavior of your family, and they enhance your reputation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.3 - I know that even until now, no one taking birth in your family has been poor-minded or miserly. No one has refused to give charity to br\'e4hma\'eb
as, nor after promising to give charity has anyone failed to fulfill his promise.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.4 - O King Bali, never in your dynasty has the low-minded King been born who upon being requested has refused charity to br\'e4hma\'ebas in holy places or a fight to k\'f1
atriyas on a battlefield. And your dynasty is even more glorious due to the presence of Prahl\'e4da Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, who is like the beautiful moon in the sky.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.5 - It was in your dynasty that Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a was born. Carrying only his own club, he wandered the globe alone, without assistance, to conquer all directions, and no hero he met could rival him.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.6 - When delivering the earth from the Garbhodaka Sea, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, in His incarnation as a boar, killed Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a, who had appeared before Him. The fight was severe, and the Lord killed Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1
a with great difficulty. Later, as the Lord thought about the uncommon prowess of Hira\'eby\'e4k\'f1a, He felt Himself victorious indeed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.7 - When Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu heard the news of his brother's being killed, with great anger he went to the residence of Vi\'f1\'ebu, the killer of his brother, wanting to kill Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.8 - Seeing Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu coming forward bearing a trident in his hand like personified death, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, the best of all mystics and the knower of the progress of time, thought as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.9 - Wheresoever I go, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu will follow Me, as death follows all living entities. Therefore it is better for Me to enter the core of his heart, for then, because of his power to see only externally, he will not see Me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.10 - Lord V\'e4manadeva continued: O King of the demons, after Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu made this decision, He entered the body of His enemy Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu, who was running after Him with great force. In a subtle body inconceivable to Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, who was in great anxiety, entered Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's nostril along with his breath.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.11 - Upon seeing that the residence of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu was vacant, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu began searching for Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu everywhere. Angry at not seeing Him, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7
ipu screamed loudly and searched the entire universe, including the surface of the earth, the higher planetary systems, all directions and all the caves and oceans. But Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, the greatest hero, did not see Vi\'f1\'ebu anywhere.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.12 - Unable to see Him, Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu said, "I have searched the entire universe, but I could not find Vi\'f1\'eb
u, who has killed my brother. Therefore, He must certainly have gone to that place from which no one returns. [In other words, He must now be dead.]"
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.13 - Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's anger against Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu persisted until his death. Other people in the bodily concept of life maintain anger only because of false ego and the great influence of ignorance.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.14 - Your father, Virocana, the son of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Prahl\'e4da, was very affectionate toward br\'e4hma\'ebas. Although he knew very well that it was the demigods who had come to him in the dress of br\'e4hma\'eb
as, at their request he delivered to them the duration of his life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.15 - You also have observed the principles followed by great personalities who are householder br\'e4hma\'ebas, by your forefathers and by great heroes who are extremely famous for their exalted activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.16 - O King of the Daityas, from Your Majesty, who come from such a noble family and who are able to give charity munificently, I ask only three paces of land, to the measurement of My steps.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.17 - O King, controller of the entire universe, although you are very munificent and are able to give Me as much land as I want, I do not want anything from you that is unnecessary. If a learned br\'e4hma\'eba takes chari
ty from others only according to his needs, he does not become entangled in sinful activities.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.18 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja said: O son of a br\'e4hma\'eba, Your instructions are as good as those of learned and elderly persons. Nonetheless, You are a boy, and You
r intelligence is insufficient. Thus You are not very prudent in regard to Your self-interest.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.19 - I am able to give You an entire island because I am the proprietor of the three divisions of the universe. You have come to take something from me 
and have pleased me by Your sweet words, but You are asking only three paces of land. Therefore You are not very intelligent.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.20 - O small boy, one who approaches me to beg something should not have to ask anything more, anywhere. Therefore, if You wish, You may ask from me as much land as will suffice to maintain You according to Your needs.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.21 - The Personality of Godhead said: O my dear King, even the entirety of whatever there may be within the three worlds to satisfy one's senses cannot satisfy a person whose senses are uncontrolled.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.22 - If I were not satisfied with three paces of land, then surely I would not be satisfied even with possessing one of the seven islands, consisting of nine var\'f1as. Even if I possessed one island
, I would hope to get others.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.23 - We have heard that although powerful kings like Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5thu and Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Gaya achieved proprietorship over the seven dv\'e9pas, they could not achieve satisfaction or find the end of their ambitions.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.24 - One should be satisfied with whatever he achieves by his previous destiny, for discontent can never bring happiness. A person who is not self-controlled will not be happy even with possessing the three worlds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.25 - Material existence causes disc
ontent in regard to fulfilling one's lusty desires and achieving more and more money. This is the cause for the continuation of material life, which is full of repeated birth and death. But one who is satisfied by that which is obtained by destiny is fit 
for liberation from this material existence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.26 - A br\'e4hma\'eba who is satisfied with whatever is providentially obtained is increasingly enlightened with spiritual power, but the spiritual potency of a dissatisfied br\'e4hma\'eba decreases, as fire diminis
hes in potency when water is sprinkled upon it.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.27 - Therefore, O King, from you, the best of those who give charity, I ask only three paces of land. By such a gift I shall be very pleased, for the way of happiness is to be fully satisfied to rece
ive that which is absolutely needed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.28 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When the Supreme Personality of Godhead had thus spoken to Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, Bali smiled and told Him, "All right. Take whatever You like." To confirm his promise to give V\'e4
manadeva the desired land, he then took up his waterpot.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.29 - Understanding Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu's purpose, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, the best of the learned, immediately spoke as follows to his disciple, who was about to offer everything to Lord V\'e4manadeva.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.30 - \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya said: O son of Virocana, this brahmac\'e4r\'e9 in the form of a dwarf is directly the imperishable Supreme Personality of Godhead, Vi\'f1\'ebu. Accepting Ka\'e7
yapa Muni as His father and Aditi as His mother, He has now appeared in order to fulfill the interests of the demigods.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.31 - You do not know what a dangerous position you have accepted by promising to give Him land. I do not think that this promise is good for you. It will bring great harm to the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.32 - This person falsely appearing as a brahmac\'e4r\'e9
 is actually the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, who has come in this form to take away all your land, wealth, beauty, power, fame and education. After taking everything from you, He will deliver it to Indra, your enemy.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.33 - Yo
u have promised to give Him three steps of land in charity, but when you give it He will occupy the three worlds. You are a rascal! You do not know what a great mistake you have made. After giving everything to Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, you will have no means of livelihood. How then shall you live?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.34 - V\'e4manadeva will first occupy the three worlds with one step, then He will take His second step and occupy everything in outer space, and then He will expand His universal body to occupy everything. Where will you
 offer Him the third step?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.35 - You will certainly be unable to fulfill your promise, and I think that because of this inability your eternal residence will be in hell.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.36 - Learned scholars do not praise that charity which endangers one'
s own livelihood. Charity, sacrifice, austerity and fruitive activities are possible for one who is competent to earn his livelihood properly. [They are not possible for one who cannot maintain himself.]
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.37 - Therefore one who is in full knowledge
 should divide his accumulated wealth in five parts-for religion, for reputation, for opulence, for sense gratification and for the maintenance of his family members. Such a person is happy in this world and in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.38 - One might argue that since you have already promised, how can you refuse? O best of the demons, just take from me the evidence of the Bahv\'e5ca-\'e7ruti, which says that a promise is truthful preceded by the word o
\'e0 and untruthful if not.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.39 - The Vedas enjoin that the fac
tual result of the tree of the body is the good fruits and flowers derived from it. But if the bodily tree does not exist, there is no possibility of factual fruits and flowers. Even if the body is based on untruth, there cannot be factual fruits and flow
ers without the help of the bodily tree.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.40 - When a tree is uprooted it immediately falls down and begins to dry up. Similarly, if one doesn't take care of the body, which is supposed to be untruth-in other words, if the untruth is uprooted-the bo
dy undoubtedly becomes dry.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.41 - The utterance of the word o\'e0 signifies separation from one's monetary assets. In other words, by uttering this word one becomes free from attachment to money because his money is taken away from him. To be without 
money is not very satisfactory, for in that position one cannot fulfill one's desires. In other words, by using the word o\'e0
 one becomes poverty-stricken. Especially when one gives charity to a poor man or beggar, one remains unfulfilled in self-realization and in sense gratification.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.42 - Therefore, the safe course is to say no. Although it is a falsehood, it protects one completely, it draws the compassion of others toward oneself, and it gives one full facility to collect money from others for on
eself. Nonetheless, if one always pleads that he has nothing, he is condemned, for he is a dead body while living, or while still breathing he should be killed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.43 - In flattering a woman to bring her under control, in joking, in a marriage ceremo
ny, in earning one's livelihood, when one's life is in danger, in protecting cows and brahminical culture, or in protecting a person from an enemy's hand, falsity is never condemned.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
