\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.18 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja said: O son of a br\'e4hma\'eba
, Your instructions are as good as those of learned and elderly persons. Nonetheless, You are a boy, and Your intelligence is insufficient. Thus You are not very prudent in regard to Your self-interest.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.19 - I am able to give You an entire island 
because I am the proprietor of the three divisions of the universe. You have come to take something from me and have pleased me by Your sweet words, but You are asking only three paces of land. Therefore You are not very intelligent.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.20 - O small boy, one who approaches me to beg something should not have to ask anything more, anywhere. Therefore, if You wish, You may ask from me as much land as will suffice to maintain You according to Your needs.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.21 - The Personality of Godhead said: O my dear King, even the entirety of whatever there may be within the three worlds to satisfy one's senses cannot satisfy a person whose senses are uncontrolled.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.22 - If I were not satisfied with three paces of land, then surely I would not be satisfied even with possessing one of the seven islands, consisting of nine var\'f1
as. Even if I possessed one island, I would hope to get others.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.23 - We have heard that although powerful kings like Mah\'e4r\'e4ja P\'e5thu and Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Gaya achieved proprietorship over the seven dv\'e9pas, they could not achieve satisfaction or find the end of their ambitions.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.24 - One should be satisfied with whatever he achieves by his previous destiny, for discontent can never bring happiness. A person who is not self-controlled will not be happy even with possessing the three worlds.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.25 - Material existence causes discontent in regard to fulfilling one's lusty desires and achieving more and more money. This is the cause for the continuation of material life, which
 is full of repeated birth and death. But one who is satisfied by that which is obtained by destiny is fit for liberation from this material existence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.26 - A br\'e4hma\'eba who is satisfied with whatever is providentially obtained is increasingly enlightened with spiritual power, but the spiritual potency of a dissatisfied br\'e4hma\'eb
a decreases, as fire diminishes in potency when water is sprinkled upon it.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.27 - Therefore, O King, from you, the best of those who give charity, I ask only three pace
s of land. By such a gift I shall be very pleased, for the way of happiness is to be fully satisfied to receive that which is absolutely needed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.28 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When the Supreme Personality of Godhead had thus spoken to Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, Bali smiled and told Him, "All right. Take whatever You like." To confirm his promise to give V\'e4
manadeva the desired land, he then took up his waterpot.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.29 - Understanding Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu's purpose, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, the best of the learned, immediately spoke as follows to his disciple, who was about to offer everything to Lord V\'e4manadeva.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.30 - \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya said: O son of Virocana, this brahmac\'e4r\'e9 in the form of a dwarf is directly the imperishable Supreme Personality of Godhead, Vi\'f1\'ebu. Accepting Ka\'e7
yapa Muni as His father and Aditi as His mother, He has now appeared in order to fulfill the interests of the demigods.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.31 - You do not know what a dangerous position you have accepted by promising to give Him land. I do not think that this promise is good for you. It will bring great harm to the demons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.32 - This person falsely appearing as a brahmac\'e4r\'e9 is actually the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, who has come in this form to take away all your land, wealth, beauty, power, fam
e and education. After taking everything from you, He will deliver it to Indra, your enemy.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.33 - You have promised to give Him three steps of land in charity, but when you give it He will occupy the three worlds. You are a rascal! You do not know 
what a great mistake you have made. After giving everything to Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, you will have no means of livelihood. How then shall you live?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.34 - V\'e4manadeva will first occupy the three worlds with one step, then He will take His second step and occup
y everything in outer space, and then He will expand His universal body to occupy everything. Where will you offer Him the third step?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.35 - You will certainly be unable to fulfill your promise, and I think that because of this inability your eternal residence will be in hell.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.36 - Learned scholars do not praise that charity which endangers one's own livelihood. Charity, sacrifice, austerity and fruitive activities are possible for one who is competent to earn his livelihood properly. [They
 are not possible for one who cannot maintain himself.]
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.37 - Therefore one who is in full knowledge should divide his accumulated wealth in five parts-for religion, for reputation, for opulence, for sense gratification and for the maintenance of h
is family members. Such a person is happy in this world and in the next.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.38 - One might argue that since you have already promised, how can you refuse? O best of the demons, just take from me the evidence of the Bahv\'e5ca-\'e7ruti, which says that a promise is truthful preceded by the word o
\'e0 and untruthful if not.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.39 - The Vedas enjoin that the factual result of the tree of the body is the good fruits and flowers derived from it. But if the bodily tree does not exist, there is no possibility o
f factual fruits and flowers. Even if the body is based on untruth, there cannot be factual fruits and flowers without the help of the bodily tree.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.40 - When a tree is uprooted it immediately falls down and begins to dry up. Similarly, if one does
n't take care of the body, which is supposed to be untruth-in other words, if the untruth is uprooted-the body undoubtedly becomes dry.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.41 - The utterance of the word o\'e0 signifies separation from one's monetary assets. In other words, by uttering 
this word one becomes free from attachment to money because his money is taken away from him. To be without money is not very satisfactory, for in that position one cannot fulfill one's desires. In other words, by using the word o\'e0
 one becomes poverty-stricken. Especially when one gives charity to a poor man or beggar, one remains unfulfilled in self-realization and in sense gratification.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.19.42 - Therefore, the safe course is to say no. Although it is a falsehood, it protects one completely, it draws
 the compassion of others toward oneself, and it gives one full facility to collect money from others for oneself. Nonetheless, if one always pleads that he has nothing, he is condemned, for he is a dead body while living, or while still breathing he shou
ld be killed.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 
SB 8.19.43 - In flattering a woman to bring her under control, in joking, in a marriage ceremony, in earning one's livelihood, when one's life is in danger, in protecting cows and brahminical culture, or in protecting a person from an enemy's
 hand, falsity is never condemned.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Translations 1-43
\par }\pard \s97\ql \fi-605\li720\ri720\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20\brdrcf14 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \tx1080\faauto\rin720\lin720\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid4605116\charrsid4605116 \chftn 
{\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat16 \b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10883103\charrsid4605116 \chftn }{
\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.1 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, when Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was thus advised by his spiritual master, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4
rya, his family priest, he remained silent for some time, and then, after full deliberation, he replied to his spiritual master as follows.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.2 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja said: As you have already stated, the principle of religion that does not hinder one's economic development, sense gratification, fame and means of livelihood is the real occupational
 duty of the householder. I also think that this religious principle is correct.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.3 - I am the grandson of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Prahl\'e4da. How can I withdraw my promise because of greed for money when I have already said that I shall give this land? How can I b
ehave like an ordinary cheater, especially toward a br\'e4hma\'eba?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.4 - There is nothing more sinful than untruthfulness. Because of this, mother earth once said, "I can bear any heavy thing except a person who is a liar."
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.5 - I do not fear hell, poverty, an ocean of distress, falldown from my position or even death itself as much as I fear cheating a br\'e4hma\'eba.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.6 - My lord, you can also see that all the material opulences of this world are certainly separated from their possessor at death. Therefore, if the br\'e4hma\'eba V\'e4
manadeva is not satisfied by whatever gifts one has given, why not please Him with the riches one is destined to lose at death?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.7 - Dadh\'e9ci, \'c7ibi and many other great personalities were willing to sacrifice even th
eir lives for the benefit of the people in general. This is the evidence of history. So why not give up this insignificant land? What is the serious consideration against it?
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.8 - O best of the br\'e4hma\'ebas, certainly the great demoniac kings who were
 never reluctant to fight enjoyed this world, but in due course of time everything they had was taken away, except their reputation, by which they continue to exist. In other words, one should try to achieve a good reputation instead of anything else.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.9 - O best of the br\'e4hma\'eb
as, many men have laid down their lives on the battlefield, being unafraid of fighting, but rarely has one gotten the chance to give his accumulated wealth faithfully to a saintly person who creates holy places.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.10 - By giving charity, a benevolent and merciful person undoubtedly becomes even more auspicious, especially when he gives charity to a person like your good self. Under the circumstances, I must give this little brahmac
\'e4r\'e9 whatever charity He wants from me.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.11 - O great sage, great saintly persons like you, being completely aware of the Vedic principles for performing ritualistic ceremonies and yaj\'efas, worship Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u in all circumstances. Therefore, whether that same Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu has come here to give me all benedictions or to punish me as an enemy, I must carry out His order and give Him the requested tract of land without hesitation.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.12 - Although He is Vi\'f1\'ebu Himself, out of fear He has covered Himself in the form of a br\'e4hma\'eba to come to me begging. Under the circumstances, because He has assumed the form of a br\'e4hma\'eb
a, even if He irreligiously arrests me or even kills me, I shall not retaliate, although He is my enemy.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.13 - If this br\'e4hma\'eba really is Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, who is worshiped by Vedic hymns, He would never give up His widespread reputation; either He would lie down having been killed by me, or He would kill me in a fight.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.14 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Thereafter, the spiritual master, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, being inspired by the Supreme Lord, cursed his exalted disciple Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, who was so magnanimous and fixed in truthfulness that instead of respecting his spiritual master's instructions, he wanted to disobey his order.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.15 - Although you have no knowledge, you have become a so-called learned person, and therefore you dare be so impudent as to disobey my order. Because of disobeying me, you shall very soon be bereft of all your opulence.

\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.16 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: Even after being cursed in this way by his own spiritual master, Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, being a great personality, never deviated from his determination. Therefore, according to custom, he first offered water to V\'e4manadeva and then offered Him the gift of land he had promised.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.17 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's wife, known as Vindhy\'e4
vali, who was decorated with a necklace of pearls, immediately came and had a large golden waterpot brought there, full of water with which to worship the Lord by washing His feet.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.18 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, the worshiper of Lord V\'e4manadeva, jubilantly washed the Lord's lotus feet and then took the water on his head, for that water delivers the entire universe.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.19 - At that time, the residents of the higher planetary system, namely the demigods, the Gandharvas, the Vidy\'e4dharas, the Siddhas and the C\'e4ra\'ebas, all being very pleased by Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja's simple, nonduplicitous act, praised his qualities and showered upon him millions of flowers.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.20 - The Gandharvas, the Kimpuru\'f1as and the Kinnaras sounded thousands and thousands of kettledrums and trumpets again and again, and they sang in great jubilation, declaring, "How exalted a person is Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, and what a difficult task he has performed! Even though he knew that Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu was on the side of his enemies, he nonetheless gave the Lord the entire three worlds in charity."
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.21 - The unlimited Supreme Personality of Godhead, who had assumed the form of V\'e4mana, then began increasing in size, acting in terms of the material energy, until everything 
in the universe was within His body, including the earth, the planetary systems, the sky, the directions, the various holes in the universe, the seas, the oceans, the birds, beasts, human beings, the demigods and the great saintly persons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.22 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, along with all the priests, \'e4c\'e4
ryas and members of the assembly, observed the Supreme Personality of Godhead's universal body, which was full of six opulences. That body contained everything within the universe, including all the gross materia
l elements, the senses, the sense objects, the mind, intelligence and false ego, the various kinds of living entities, and the actions and reactions of the three modes of material nature.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.23 - Thereafter, Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, who was occupying the seat of King Indra, could see the lower planetary systems, such as Ras\'e4
tala, on the soles of the feet of the Lord's universal form. He saw on the Lord's feet the surface of the globe, on the surface of His calves all the mountains, on His knees the various birds, and on His thighs the varieties of air.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.24 - Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja saw beneath the garments of the Lord, who acts wonderfully, the evening twilight. In the Lord's private parts he saw the Praj\'e4patis, and in the round portion of the waist he saw himself
 with his confidential associates. In the Lord's navel he saw the sky, on the Lord's waist he saw the seven oceans, and on the Lord's bosom he saw all the clusters of stars.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.25-29 - My dear King, on the heart of Lord Mur\'e4ri he saw religion; on the
 chest, both pleasing words and truthfulness; in the mind, the moon; on the bosom, the goddess of fortune, with a lotus flower in her hand; on the neck, all the Vedas and all sound vibrations; on the arms, all the demigods, headed by King Indra; in both e
ars, all the directions; on the head, the upper planetary systems; on the hair, the clouds; in the nostrils, the wind; on the eyes, the sun; and in the mouth, fire. From His words came all the Vedic mantras, on His tongue was the demigod of water, Varu
\'ebadev
a, on His eyebrows were the regulative principles, and on His eyelids were day and night. [When His eyes were open it was daytime, and when they were closed it was night.] On His forehead was anger, and on His lips was greed. O King, in His touch were lus
t
y desires, in His semen were all the waters, on His back was irreligion, and in His wonderful activities or steps was the fire of sacrifice. On His shadow was death, in His smile was the illusory energy, and on the hairs of His body were all the drugs and
 herbs. In His veins were all the rivers, on His nails were all the stones, in His intelligence were Lord Brahm\'e4
, the demigods and the great saintly persons, and throughout His entire body and senses were all living entities, moving and stationary. Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja thus saw everything in the gigantic body of the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.30 - O King, when all the demons, the followers of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Bali, saw the universal form of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who held everything within His body, when they saw in the L
ord's hand His disc, known as the Sudar\'e7ana cakra, which generates intolerable heat, and when they heard the tumultuous sound of His bow, all of these caused lamentation within their hearts.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.31 - The Lord's conchshell, named P\'e4\'efcajanya, which made sounds like that of a cloud; the very forceful club named Kaumodak\'e9; the sword named Vidy\'e4
dhara, with a shield decorated with hundreds of moonlike spots; and also Ak\'f1ayas\'e4yaka, the best of quivers-all of these appeared together to offer prayers to the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 
SB 8.20.32-33 - These associates, headed by Sunanda and other chief associates and accompanied by all the predominating deities of the various planets, offered prayers to the Lord, who wore a brilliant helmet, bracelets, and glittering earrings that re
sembled fish. On the Lord's bosom were the lock of hair called \'c7r\'e9
vatsa and the transcendental jewel named Kaustubha. He wore a yellow garment, covered by a belt, and He was decorated by a flower garland, surrounded by bees. Manifesting Himself in this way
, O King, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, whose activities are wonderful, covered the entire surface of the earth with one footstep, the sky with His body, and all directions with His arms.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.20.34 - As the Lord took His second step, He covered the
 heavenly planets. And not even a spot remained for the third step, for the Lord's foot extended higher and higher, beyond Maharloka, Janaloka, Tapoloka and even Satyaloka.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  20. }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid4605116\charrsid4605116 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
