\bullet   }{\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid4605116\charrsid4605116 \chftn {\footnote\ftnalt \pard\plain \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20\brdrcf13 \faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\b\f35\fs24\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs1867\fs14\up12\insrsid10883103\charrsid4605116 \chftn }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.1 - Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1
it said: The Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, is eternally situated in His transcendental position, yet He descends to this material world and manifests Himself in various incarnations. His first incarnation was that of a great fish. O most powerful 
\'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, I wish to hear from you the pastimes of that fish incarnation.
\par }\pard \s1217\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.2-3 - What was the purpose for which the 
Supreme Personality of Godhead accepted the abominable form of a fish, exactly as an ordinary living being accepts different forms under the laws of karma? The form of a fish is certainly condemned and full of terrible pain. O my lord, what was the purpos
e of this incarnation? Kindly explain this to us, for hearing about the pastimes of the Lord is auspicious for everyone.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.4 - S\'fcta Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: When Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja thus inquired from \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9, that most powerful saintly person began describing the pastimes of the Lord's incarnation as a fish.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.5 - \'c7r\'e9 \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 said: O King, for the sake of protecting the cows, br\'e4hma\'ebas, demigods, devotees, the Vedic literature, religious principles, and principles to fulfill the purpose o
f life, the Supreme Personality of Godhead accepts the forms of incarnations.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.6 - Like the air passing through different types of atmosphere, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, although appearing sometimes as a human being and sometimes as a lowe
r animal, is always transcendental. Because He is above the material modes of nature, He is unaffected by higher and lower forms.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.7 - O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, at the end of the past millennium, at the end of Brahm\'e4's day, because Lord Brahm\'e4 sleeps during
 the night, annihilation took place, and the three worlds were covered by the water of the ocean.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.8 - At the end of Brahm\'e4's day, when Brahm\'e4 felt sleepy and desired to lie down, the Vedas were emanating from his mouth, and the great demon named Hayagr\'e9va stole the Vedic knowledge.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.9 - Understanding the acts of the great demon Hayagr\'e9va, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Hari, who is full of all opulences, assumed the form of a fish and saved the Vedas by killing the demon.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.10 - During the C\'e4k\'f1u\'f1a-manvantara there was a great king named Satyavrata who was a great devotee of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Satyavrata performed austerities by subsisting only on water.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.11 - In this [the present] millennium King Satyavrata later became the son of Vivasv\'e4n, the king of the sun planet, and was known as \'c7r\'e4
ddhadeva. By the mercy of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, he was given the post of Manu.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.12 - One day while King Satyavrata was performing austerities by offering water on the bank of the River K\'e5tam\'e4l\'e4, a small fish appeared in the water in his palms.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.13 - Satyavrata, the King of Dravi\'f2ade\'e7a, threw the fish into the water of the river along with the water in his palm, O King Par\'e9k\'f1it, descendant of Bharata.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 
SB 8.24.14 - With an appealing voice, the poor small fish said to King Satyavrata, who was very merciful: My dear King, protector of the poor, why are you throwing Me in the water of the river, where there are other aquatics who can kill Me? I am v
ery much afraid of them.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.15 - To please himself, King Satyavrata, not knowing that the fish was the Supreme Personality of Godhead, decided with great pleasure to give the fish protection.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.16 - The merciful King, being moved by the pitiable words of the fish, placed the fish in a water jug and brought Him to his own residence.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.17 - But in one night that fish grew so much that He could not move His body comfortably in the water of the pot. He then spoke to the King as follows.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.18 - O My dear King, I do not like living in this waterpot with such great difficulty. Therefore, please find some better reservoir of water where I can live comfortably.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.19 - Then, taking the fish out of the waterpot, the King threw Him in a large well. But within a moment the fish developed to the length of three cubits.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.20 - The fish then said: My dear King, this reservoir of water is not fit for My happy residence. Please give Me a more extensive pool of water, for I have taken shelter of you.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
