u, who had assumed the form of a fish.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.41 - Thereafter, gigantic clouds pouring incessant water swelled the ocean more and more. Thus the ocean began to overflow onto the land and inundate the entire world.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.42 - As Satyavrata remembered the order of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, he saw a boat coming near him. Thus he collected herbs and creepers, and, accompanied by saintly br\'e4hma\'ebas, he got aboard the boat.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.43 - The saintly br\'e4hma\'ebas, being pleased with the King, said to him: O King, please meditate upon the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Ke\'e7
ava. He will save us from this impending danger and arrange for our well-being.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.44 - Then, while the King constantly meditated upon the Supreme Personality of Godhead, a large golden fish appeared in the ocean of inundation. The fish had one horn and was eight million miles long.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.45 - Following the instructions formerly given by the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the King anchored the boat to the fish's horn, using the serpent V\'e4
suki as a rope. Thus being satisfied, he began offering prayers to the Lord.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.46 - The King said: By the grace of the Lord, those who have lost their self-knowledge since time immemorial, and who because
 of this ignorance are involved in a material, conditional life full of miseries, obtain the chance to meet the Lord's devotee. I accept that Supreme Personality of Godhead as the supreme spiritual master.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.47 - In hopes of becoming happy in this m
aterial world, the foolish conditioned soul performs fruitive activities that result only in suffering. But by rendering service to the Supreme Personality of Godhead, one becomes free from such false desires for happiness. May my supreme spiritual master
 cut the knot of false desires from the core of my heart.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.48 - One who wants to be free of material entanglement should take to the service of the Supreme Personality of Godhead and give up the contamination of ignorance, involving pious and impiou
s activities. Thus one regains his original identity, just as a block of gold or silver sheds all dirt and becomes purified when treated with fire. May that inexhaustible Supreme Personality of Godhead become our spiritual master, for He is the original s
piritual master of all other spiritual masters.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.49 - Neither all the demigods, nor the so-called gurus nor all other people, either independently or together, can offer mercy that equals even one ten-thousandth of Yours. Therefore I wish to take sh
elter of Your lotus feet.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.50 - As a blind man, being unable to see, accepts another blind man as his leader, people who do not know the goal of life accept someone as a guru who is a rascal and a fool. But we are interested in self-realization. Th
erefore we accept You, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, as our spiritual master, for You are able to see in all directions and are omniscient like the sun.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.51 - A materialistic so-called guru instructs his materialistic disciples about economic
 development and sense gratification, and because of such instructions the foolish disciples continue in the materialistic existence of ignorance. But Your Lordship gives knowledge that is eternal, and the intelligent person receiving such knowledge is qu
ickly situated in his original constitutional position.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.52 - My Lord, You are the supreme well-wishing friend of everyone, the dearmost friend, the controller, the Supersoul, the supreme instructor and the giver of supreme knowledge and the fulfill
ment of all desires. But although You are within the heart, the foolish, because of lusty desires in the heart, cannot understand You.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.53 - O Supreme Lord, for self-realization I surrender unto You, who are worshiped by the demigods as the supreme
 controller of everything. By Your instructions, exposing life's purpose, kindly cut the knot from the core of my heart and let me know the destination of my life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.54 - \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 continued: When Satyavrata had thus prayed to the Supreme Per
sonality of Godhead, who had assumed the form of a fish, the Lord, while moving in the water of inundation, explained to him the Absolute Truth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.55 - The Supreme Personality of Godhead thus explained to King Satyavrata the spiritual science known as s\'e4\'ec
khya-yoga, the science by which one distinguishes between matter and spirit [in other words, bhakti-yoga], along with the instructions contained in the Pur\'e4\'ebas [the old histories] and the sa\'e0hit\'e4
s. The Lord explained Himself in all these literatures.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 
SB 8.24.56 - While sitting in the boat, King Satyavrata, accompanied by the great saintly persons, listened to the instructions of the Supreme Personality of Godhead in regard to self-realization. These instructions were all from the eternal Vedic litera
ture [brahma]. Thus the King and sages had no doubt about the Absolute Truth.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.57 - At the end of the last inundation [during the period of Sv\'e4yambhuva Manu] the Supreme Personality of Godhead killed the demon named Hayagr\'e9va and delivered all the Vedic literatures to Lord Brahm\'e4
 when Lord Brahm\'e4 awakened from sleeping.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.58 - King Satyavrata was illuminated with all Vedic knowledge by the mercy of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, and in this period he has now taken birth as Vaivasvata Manu, the son of the sun-god.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.59 - This story concerning the great King Satyavrata and the fish incarnation of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Vi\'f1\'eb
u, is a great transcendental narration. Anyone who hears it is delivered from the reactions of sinful life.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.60 - One who narrates this description of the Matsya incarnation and King Satyavrata will certainly have all his ambitions fulfilled, and he will undoubtedly return home, back to Godhead.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }{\fs28\insrsid10883103 SB 8.24.61 - I offer my respectful obeisances unto the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who pretended to be a gigantic fish, who restored the Vedic literature to Lord Brahm\'e4 when Lord Brahm\'e4
 awakened from sleep, and who explained the essence of Vedic literature to King Satyavrata and the great saintly persons.
\par }{\b0\cf0\insrsid10883103 
\par }}}{\f35\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Translations 1-61
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.1: The Manus, Administrators of the Universe
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\insrsid15153253 CHAPTER ONE\line }{\insrsid4605116 The Manus, Administrators of the Universe
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.1 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid4418615 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 
First of all, let me offer my humble, respectful obeisances unto the lotus feet of my spiritual master, His Divine Grace \'c7r\'e9 \'c7r\'e9mad Bhaktisiddh\'e4nta Sarasvat\'e9 Gosv\'e4m\'e9 Prabhup\'e4
da. Sometime in the year 1935 when His Divine Grace was staying at R\'e4dh\'e4-ku\'eb\'f2a, I went to see him from Bombay. At that time, he gave me many important instructions in regard to constructing tem
ples and publishing books. He personally told me that publishing books is more important than constructing temples. Of course, those same instructions remained within my mind for many years. In 1944 I began publishing my }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 
Back to Godhead,}{\insrsid4605116  and when I retired from family life in 1958 I began publishing }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{\insrsid4605116  in Delhi. When three parts of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{
\insrsid4605116  had been published in India, I then started for the United States of America on the thirteenth of August, 1965.
\par }\pard \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid4605116 I am continuously trying to publish books, as suggested by my spiritual master. Now, in this year, 1976, I have completed the Seventh Canto of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 
\'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\insrsid4605116  and a summary of the Tenth Canto has already been published as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 K\'e5\'f1\'eba, the Supreme Personality of Godhead.}{\insrsid4605116  Still, the Eighth Can
to, Ninth Canto, Tenth Canto, Eleventh Canto and Twelfth Canto are yet to be published. On this occasion, therefore, I am praying to my spiritual master to give me strength to finish this work. I am neither a great scholar nor a great devotee; I am simply
 
a humble servant of my spiritual master, and to the best of my ability I am trying to please him by publishing these books, with the cooperation of my disciples in America. Fortunately, scholars all over the world are appreciating these publications. Let 
us cooperatively publish more and more volumes of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam}{\insrsid4605116  just to please His Divine Grace Bhaktisiddh\'e4nta Sarasvat\'e9 \'d6h\'e4kura.
\par This First Chapter of the Eighth Canto may be summarized as a description of four Manus, namely Sv\'e4yambhuva, Sv\'e4roci\'f1a, Uttama and T\'e4masa. After hearing descriptions of the dynasty of Sv\'e4yambhuva Manu until the end of the Seventh Canto, Mah
\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it desired to know about other Manus. He desired to understand how the Supreme Personality of Godhead descends-not only in the past but at the present and in the future-and how He acts in various pastimes as Manu. Since Par\'e9k
\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja was eager to know all this, \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 gradually described all the Manus, beginning with the six Manus who had appeared in the past.
\par The first Manu was Sv\'e4yambhuva Manu. His two daughters, namely \'c4k\'fcti and Devah\'fcti, gave birth to two sons, named Yaj\'efa and Kapila respectively. Because \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9
 had already described the activities of Kapila in the Third Canto, he now described the activities of Yaj\'efa. The original Manu, along with his wife, \'c7atar\'fcp\'e4, went into the forest to practice austerities on the bank of the River Sunand\'e4
. They practiced austerities for a hundred years, and then Manu, in a trance, formed prayers to the Supreme Personality of Godhead. R\'e4k\'f1asas and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 asuras}{\insrsid4605116  then attempted to devour him, but Yaj\'ef
a, accompanied by his sons the Y\'e4mas and the demigods, killed them. Then Yaj\'efa personally took the post of Indra, the King of the heavenly planets.
\par The second Manu, whose name was Sv\'e4roci\'f1a, was the son of Agni, and His sons were headed by Dyumat, Su\'f1e\'eba and Roci\'f1
mat. In the age of this Manu, Rocana became Indra, the ruler of the heavenly planets, and there were many demigods, headed by Tu\'f1ita. There were also many saintly persons, such as \'dcrja and Stambha. Among them was Veda\'e7ir\'e4, whose wife, Tu\'f1it
\'e4, gave birth to Vibhu. Vibhu instructed eighty-eight thousand }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 d\'e5\'f2ha-vratas,}{\insrsid4605116  or saintly persons, on self-control and austerity.
\par Uttama, the son of Priyavrata, was the third Manu. Among his sons were Pavana, S\'e5\'efjaya and Yaj\'efahotra. During the reign of this Manu, the sons of Vasi\'f1\'f6ha, headed by Pramada, became the seven saintly persons. The Satyas, Deva\'e7
rutas and Bhadras became the demigods, and Satyajit became Indra. From the womb of Sun\'e5t\'e4, the wife of Dharma, the Lord appeared as Satyasena, and He killed all the Yak\'f1as and R\'e4k\'f1asas who were fighting with Satyajit.
\par T\'e4masa, the brother of the third Manu, was the fourth Manu, and he had ten sons, including P\'e5thu, Khy\'e4ti, Nara and Ketu. During his reign, the Satyakas, Haris, V\'e9ras and others were demigods, the seven great saints were headed by Jyotirdh\'e4
ma, and Tri\'e7ikha became Indra. Harimedh\'e4 begot a son named Hari in the womb of his wife Hari\'eb\'e9. This Hari, an incarnation of God, saved the devotee Gajendra. This incident is described as }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 gajendra-mok\'f1a\'eba.}{
\insrsid4605116  At the end of this chapter, Par\'e9k\'f1it Mah\'e4r\'e4ja particularly asks about this incident.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.1.1
