ocodile, out of the water. Then, in the presence of all the demigods, who were looking on, the Lord severed the crocodile's mouth from its body with His disc. In this way He saved Gajendra, the King of the elephants.
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Eighth Canto, Third Chapter, of the }{
