\par }\pard\plain \s3006\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid6049549 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 It is described that while Durv\'e4s\'e4
 Muni was passing on the road, he saw Indra on the back of his elephant and was pleased to offer Indra a garland from his own neck. Indra, however, being too puffed up, took the garland, and without respect for Durv\'e4s\'e4
 Muni, he placed it on the trunk of his carrier elephant. The elephant, being an animal, could not understand the value of the garland, and thus the elephant threw the garland between its legs and smashed it. Seeing this insulting behavior, Durv\'e4s\'e4
 Muni immediately cursed Indra to be poverty-stricken, bereft of all material opulence. Thus the demigods, afflicted on one side by the fighting demons and on the other by the curse of Durv\'e4s\'e4
 Muni, lost all the material opulences in the three worlds.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3017\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid7616644 
