\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid4605116 nirgu\'eb\'e4ya}{\cs3010\fs28\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 \emdash which are all transcendental, free from the inebriety of material qualities; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid4605116 gu\'eba-\'e9\'e7\'e4ya}{
\cs3010\fs28\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 \emdash unto You, who control the three modes of material nature; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid4605116 sattva-sth\'e4ya}{\cs3010\fs28\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 \emdash 
who are in favor of the material quality of goodness; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid4605116 ca}{\cs3010\fs28\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 \emdash also; }{\cs1998\i\fs28\insrsid4605116 s\'e4mpratam}{\cs3010\fs28\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 \emdash 
at present.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid4605116 TRANSLATION
\par }\pard\plain \s3003\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sb36\sa65\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid6049549 \b\f35\fs28\cf13\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 
My Lord, all obeisances unto You, who are eternal, beyond time's limits of past, present and future. You are inconceivable in Your activities, You are the master of the three modes of material nature, and, being transcendental to all material qualities
, You are free from material contamination. You are the controller of all three of the modes of nature, but at the present You are in favor of the quality of goodness. Let us offer our respectful obeisances unto You.
\par }\pard\plain \s1187\qc \li0\ri0\sb360\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\v\f35\fs24\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\v0\fs28\insrsid4605116 PURPORT
\par }\pard\plain \s537\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 The Supreme Personality of Godh
ead controls the material activities manifested by the three modes of material nature. As stated in }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4,}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 nirgu\'eba
\'e0 gu\'eba-bhokt\'e5 ca:}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  the Supreme Personality of Godhead is always transcendental to the material qualities (}{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 sattva-gu\'eba, rajo-gu\'eba}{
\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 tamo-gu\'eba}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 ), but nonetheless He is their controller. The Lord manifests Himself in three features\emdash as Brahm
\'e4, Vi\'f1\'ebu and Mahe\'e7vara\emdash to control these three qualities. He personally takes charge of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 sattva-gu\'eba}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  as Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u, and He entrusts the charge of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 rajo-gu\'eba}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 tamo-gu\'eba}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  to Lord Brahm\'e4
 and Lord \'c7iva. Ultimately, however, He is the controller of all three }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 gu\'ebas.}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  Lord Brahm\'e4, expressing his appreciation, said that because Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u had now taken charge of the activities of goodness, there was every hope that the demigods would be successful in fulfilling their desires. The demigods were harassed by the demons, who were infested with }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 tamo-gu\'eb
a.}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  However, as Lord Brahm\'e4 has previously described, since the time of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 sattva-gu\'eba}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549 
 had now arrived, the demigods could na
turally expect to fulfill their desires. The demigods are supposedly well advanced in knowledge, yet they could not understand the knowledge of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Therefore the Lord is addressed here as }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 anant\'e4ya.}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  Although Lord Brahm\'e4 knows past, present and future, he is unable to understand the unlimited knowledge of the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Eighth Canto, Fifth Chapter, of the }{
\cs2344\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  entitled "The Demigods Appeal to the Lord for Protection."
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.6: The Demigods and Demons Declare a Truce
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\insrsid15153253 CHAPTER SIX\line }{\insrsid4605116 The Demigods and Demons Declare a Truce
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.6 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid4418615 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 
This chapter describes how the Lord appeared before the demigods when they offered Him their prayers. Following the advice 
of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the demigods executed a truce with the demons for the purpose of churning nectar from the sea.
\par }\pard \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid4605116 Because of the prayers offered by the demigods in the previous chapter, Lord K\'f1\'e9rodaka\'e7\'e4y\'e9 Vi\'f1\'ebu was pleased with the demi
gods, and thus He appeared before them. The demigods were almost blinded by His transcendental bodily effulgence. At first, therefore, they could not even see any part of His body. After some time, however, when Brahm\'e4
 could see the Lord, he, along with Lord \'c7iva, began to offer the Lord prayers.
\par Lord Brahm\'e4 said: "The Supreme Personality of Godhead, being beyond birth and death, is eternal. He has no material qualities. Yet He is the ocean of unlimited auspicious qualities. He is subtler than the most subt
le, He is invisible, and His form is inconceivable. He is worshipable for all the demigods. Innumerable universes exist within His form, and therefore He is never separated from these universes by time, space or circumstances. He is the chief and the }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 pradh\'e4na.}{\insrsid4605116  Although He is the beginning, the middle and the end of the material creation, the idea of pantheism conceived by M\'e4y\'e4v\'e4d\'e9
 philosophers has no validity. The Supreme Personality of Godhead controls the entire material manifestation through His subordin
ate agent, the external energy. Because of His inconceivable transcendental position, He is always the master of the material energy. The Supreme Personality of Godhead, in His various forms, is always present even within this material world, but the mate
rial qualities cannot touch Him. One can understand His position only by His instructions, as given in }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4.}{\insrsid4605116 " As stated in }{\cs2542\i\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\insrsid4605116 
 (10.10), }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 dad\'e4mi buddhi-yoga\'e0 tam.}{\insrsid4605116  }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Buddhi-yoga}{\insrsid4605116  means }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 bhakti-yoga.}{\insrsid4605116  Only through the process of }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 bhakti-yoga}{\insrsid4605116  can one understand the Supreme Lord.
\par When offered prayers by Lord \'c7iva and Lord Brahm\'e4, the Supreme Personality of Godhead was pleased. Thus He gave appropriate instructions to all the demigods. The Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is known as Ajita, unconque
rable, advised the demigods to make a peace proposal to the demons, so that after formulating a truce, the demigods and demons could churn the ocean of milk. The rope would be the biggest serpent, known as V\'e4
suki, and the churning rod would be Mandara Mountain. Poison would also be produced from the churning, but it would be taken by Lord \'c7
iva, and so there would be no need to fear it. Many other attractive things would be generated by the churning, but the Lord warned the demigods not to be captivated by s
uch things. Nor should the demigods be angry if there were some disturbances. After advising the demigods in this way, the Lord disappeared from the scene.
\par Following the instructions of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the demigods established a peace with Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Bali, the King of the demons. Then both the demons and the demigods started for the ocean, taking Mandara Mountain with them. Because of the great heaviness of the mountain, the demigods and demons became fatigued, and some of them actually di
ed. Then the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Vi\'f1\'ebu, appeared there on the back of His carrier, Garu\'f2
a, and by His mercy He brought these demigods and demons back to life. The Lord then lifted the mountain with one of His hands and placed it on the back of Garu\'f2a. The Lord sat on the mountain and was carried to the spot of the churning by Garu\'f2
a, who placed the mountain in the middle of the sea. Then the Lord asked Garu\'f2a to leave that place because as long as Garu\'f2a was present, V\'e4suki could not come there.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.6.1
