a was asked by the Lord to leave that place because the snake V\'e4suki, who was to be used as the rope for churning, could not go there in the presence of Garu\'f2a. Garu\'f2a, the carrier of Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu, is not a vegetarian. He eats big snakes. V
\'e4suki, being a great snake, would be natural food for Garu\'f2a, the chief of birds. Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu therefore asked Garu\'f2a to leave so that V\'e4
suki could be brought to churn the ocean with Mandara Mountain, which was to be used as the churning rod. These are the wonderful 
arrangements of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Nothing takes place by accident. Carrying Mandara Mountain on the back of a bird and putting it in its right position might be difficult for anyone, whether demigod or demon, but for the Supreme Personal
ity of Godhead everything is possible, as shown by this pastime. The Lord had no difficulty lifting the mountain with one hand, and Garu\'f2
a, His carrier, carried all the demons and demigods together by the grace of the Supreme Lord. The Lord is known as Yoge\'e7
vara, the master of all mystic power, because of His omnipotence. If He likes, He can make anything lighter than cotton or heavier than the universe. Those who do not believe in the activities of the Lord cannot explain how things happen. Using words like
 "accident," they take shelter of ideas that are unbelievable. Nothing is accidental. Everything is done by the Supreme Personality of Godhead, as the Lord Himself confirms in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{
\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  (9.10). }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 May\'e4dhyak\'f1e\'eba prak\'e5ti\'f9 s\'fcyate sacar\'e4caram.}{\cs3008\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116\charrsid6049549  Whatever action
