 (4.11) it is said, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 ye yath\'e4 m\'e4\'e0 prapadyante t\'e4\'e0s tathaiva bhaj\'e4my aham:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 the Supreme Personality of Godhead is the supreme judge who rewards or punishes different persons according to their surrender unto His lotus feet. Therefore it can actually be seen that although }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 bhaktas}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  may work in the same place, at the same time, with the same energy and with the same ambition, they achieve different results. The }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  transmigrate through different bodies in the cycle of birth and death, sometimes going upward and sometimes downward, thus suffering the results of their actions in the }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karma-cakra,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  the cycle of birth and death. The devotees, however, because of fully surrendering at the lotus feet of the Lord, are never baffled in their attempts. Althoug
h externally they work almost like the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 the devotees go back home, back to Godhead, and achieve success in every effort. The demons or atheists have faith in their own endeavors, but although they work very hard day and night, they cannot get any mo
re than their destiny. The devotees, however, can surpass the reactions of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karma}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  and achieve wonderful results, even without effort. It is also said, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
phalena paric\'e9yate:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  one's success or defeat in any activity is understood by its result. There are many }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 in the dress of devotees, but the Supreme Personality of Godhead can detect their purpose. The }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 want to use the property of the Lord for their selfish sense gratification, but a devotee endeavors to use the Lord's property for God's service. Therefore a devotee is always distinct from the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  although the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  may dress like devotees. As confirmed in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  (3.9), }{
\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 yaj\'ef\'e4rth\'e4t karma\'ebo 'nyatra loko 'ya\'e0 karma-bandhana\'f9.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  One who works for Lord Vi\'f1\'ebu is free from this material world, and 
after giving up his body he goes back home, back to Godhead. A }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 however, although externally working like a devotee, is entangled in his nondevotional activity, and thus he suffers the tribulations of material existence. Thus from the results achieved by the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  and devotees, one can understand the presence of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who acts differently for the }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 karm\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  and }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 j
\'ef\'e4n\'e9s}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  than for the devotees. The author of }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 \'c7r\'e9 Caitanya-carit\'e4m\'e5ta}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  therefore says:
