f this narration concerning the churning of the milk ocean is clearly manifested by the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Although He is equal to everyone, because of natural affection He favors His devotees. The Lord says in }{
\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  (9.29):
\par }\pard\plain \s1462\qc \li0\ri0\sb180\sa180\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 samo 'ha\'e0 sarva-bh\'fcte\'f1u\line na me dve\'f1yo 'sti na priya\'f9\line 
ye bhajanti tu m\'e4\'e0 bhakty\'e4\line mayi te te\'f1u c\'e4py aham
\par }\pard\plain \s135\qj \li0\ri0\sb89\sa89\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
"I envy no one, nor am I partial to anyone. I am equal to all. But whoever renders service unto Me in devotion is a friend, is in Me, and I am also a friend to him.
" This partiality of the Supreme Personality of Godhead is natural. A person cares for his children not because of partiality but in a reciprocation of love. The children depend on the father's affection, and the father affectionately maintains the childr
en. Similarly, because devotees do not know anything but the lotus feet of the Lord, the Lord is always prepared to give protection to His devotees and fulfill their desires. He therefore says, }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 kaunteya pratij\'e4n\'e9
hi na me bhakta\'f9 pra\'eba\'e7yati:}{\cs1998\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  }{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 "O son of Kunt\'e9, declare it boldly that My devotee never perishes."
\par }\pard\plain \s1178\ql \li0\ri0\sb360\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \i\f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Thus end the Bhaktivedanta purports of the Eighth Canto, Twelfth Chapter, of the }{
\cs2344\i0\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 \'c7r\'e9mad-Bh\'e4gavatam,}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  entitled "The Mohin\'e9-m\'fcrti Incarnation Bewilders Lord \'c7iva."
\par }\pard\plain \s20\qr \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf18\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.13: Description of Future Manus
\par }\pard\plain \s330\qc \li0\ri0\sb540\sa540\nowidctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw105\brsp140\brdrcf6 \faauto\rin0\lin0\rtlgutter\itap0 \shading10000\cfpat10 \b\f35\fs36\cf8\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {
\insrsid4220433 CHAPTER THIRTEEN\line }{\insrsid4605116 Description of Future Manus
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.13 Summary
\par }\pard\plain \qj \li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid4418615 \f35\fs28\cf1\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 
Of the fourteen Manus, six Manus have already been described. Now, this chapter will consecutively describe each Manu from the seventh to the fourteenth.
\par }\pard \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid4605116 The seventh Manu, who is the son of Vivasv\'e4n, is known as \'c7r\'e4ddhadeva. He has ten sons, named Ik\'f1v\'e4ku, Nabhaga, Dh\'e5\'f1\'f6a, \'c7ary\'e4ti, Nari\'f1
yanta, N\'e4bh\'e4ga, Di\'f1\'f6a, Tar\'fc\'f1a, P\'e5\'f1adhra and Vasum\'e4n. In this }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 manvantara,}{\insrsid4605116  or reign of Manu, among the demigods are the \'c4dityas, Vasus, Rudras, Vi\'e7vedevas, Maruts, A\'e7vin\'e9-
kum\'e4ras and \'c5bhus. The king of heaven, Indra, is known as Purandara, and the seven sages are known as Ka\'e7yapa, Atri, Vasi\'f1\'f6ha, Vi\'e7v\'e4mitra, Gautama, Jamadagni and Bharadv\'e4
ja. During this period of Manu, the Supreme Personality of Godhead Vi\'f1\'ebu appears from the womb of Aditi in His incarnation as the son of Ka\'e7yapa.
\par In the period of the eighth Manu, the Manu is S\'e4var\'ebi. His sons are headed by Nirmoka, and among the demigods are the Sutap\'e4s. Bali, the son of Virocana, is Indra, and G\'e4lava and Para\'e7ur\'e4ma are am
ong the seven sages. In this age of Manu, the incarnation of the Supreme Personality of Godhead appears as S\'e4rvabhauma, the son of Devaguhya and Sarasvat\'e9.
\par In the period of the ninth Manu, the Manu is Dak\'f1a-s\'e4var\'ebi. His sons are headed by Bh\'fctaketu, and among the demigods are the Mar\'e9cigarbhas. Adbhuta is Indra, and among the seven sages is Dyutim\'e4n. In this period of Manu, the incarnation 
\'c5\'f1abha is born of \'c4yu\'f1m\'e4n and Ambudh\'e4r\'e4.
\par In the period of the tenth Manu, the Manu is Brahma-s\'e4var\'ebi. Among his sons is Bh\'fcri\'f1e\'eba, and the seven sages are Havi\'f1m\'e4n and others. Among the demigods are the Suv\'e4sanas, and \'c7
ambhu is Indra. The incarnation in this period of Manu is Vi\'f1vaksena, who is a friend of \'c7ambhu and who is born from the womb of Vi\'f1\'fcc\'e9 in the house of a }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 br\'e4hma\'eba}{\insrsid4605116  named Vi\'e7vasra\'f1\'f6
\'e4.
\par In the period of the eleventh Manu, the Manu is Dharma-s\'e4var\'ebi, who has ten sons, headed by Satyadharma. Among the demigods are the Viha\'ecgamas, Indra is known as Vaidh\'e5ta, and the seven sages are Aru\'eba and others. In this }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 manvantara,}{\insrsid4605116  the incarnation is Dharmasetu, who is born of Vaidh\'e5t\'e4 and \'c4ryaka.
\par In the period of the twelfth Manu, the Manu is Rudra-s\'e4var\'ebi, whose sons are headed by Devav\'e4n. The demigods are the Haritas and others, Indra is \'c5tadh\'e4m\'e4, and the seven sages are Tapom\'fcrti and others. The incarnation in this }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 manvantara}{\insrsid4605116  is Sudh\'e4m\'e4, or Svadh\'e4m\'e4, who is born from the womb of Sun\'e5t\'e4. His father's name is Satyasah\'e4.
\par }\pard \qj \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid4605116 In the period of the thirteenth Manu, the Manu is Deva-s\'e4var\'ebi. Among his sons is Citrasena, the demigods are the Sukarm\'e4
s and others, Indra is Divaspati, and Nirmoka is among the sages. The }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 manvantara-avat\'e4ra}{\insrsid4605116  is Yoge\'e7vara, who is born of Devahotra and B\'e5hat\'e9.
\par In the period of the fourteenth Manu, the Manu is Indra-s\'e4var\'ebi. Among his sons are Uru and Gambh\'e9ra, the demigods are the Pavitras and others, Indra is \'c7uci, and among the sages are Agni and B\'e4hu. The incarnation of this }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 manvantara}{\insrsid4605116  is known as B\'e5hadbh\'e4nu. He is born of Satr\'e4ya\'eba from the womb of Vit\'e4n\'e4.
\par The total duration of the periods ruled by these Manus is calculated to be one thousand }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 catur-yugas,}{\insrsid4605116  or 4,300,000 times 1,000 years.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.13.1
