\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 guru.
\par }\pard \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid4605116 Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Par\'e9k\'f1it wanted to understand how Lord V\'e4manadeva, on the plea of taking three paces of land from Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, took everything away from him and arrested him. \'c7ukadeva Gosv\'e4m\'e9 responded to this inquiry with the following explanation. In the fight between the demons and the demigods, as described in the Eleventh Chapter o
f this canto, Bali was defeated, and he died in the fight, but by the grace of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya he regained his life. Thus he engaged himself in the service of \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, his spiritual master. The descendants of Bh\'e5
gu, being pleased with him, engaged him in the Vi\'e7vajit-yaj\'efa. When this }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 yaj\'efa}{\insrsid4605116  was performed, from the fire of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 yaj\'efa}{\insrsid4605116 
 came a chariot, horses, a flag, a bow, armor and two quivers of arrows. Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Prahl\'e4da, Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's grandfather, gave Bali an eternal garland of flowers, and \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya gave him a conchshell. Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, after offering obeisances to Prahl\'e4da, the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid4605116  and his spiritual master, \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, equipped himself to fight with Indra and went to Indrapur\'e9
 with his soldiers. Blowing his conchshell, he attacked the outskirts of Indra's kingdom. When Indra saw Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja's prowess, he went to his own spiritual master, B\'e5haspati, told him about Bali's strength, and inquired about his duty. B\'e5
haspati informed the demigods that because Bali had been endowed with extraordinary power by the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\insrsid4605116 
 the demigods could not fight with him. Their only hope was to gain the favor of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. Indeed, there was no alternative. Under the circumstances, B\'e5haspati advised the demigods to leave the heavenly planets and k
eep themselves somewhere invisible. The demigods followed his orders, and Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja, along with his associates, gained the entire kingdom of Indra. The descendants of Bh\'e5gu Muni, being very affectionate to their disciple Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja, engaged him in performing one hundred }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 a\'e7vamedha-yaj\'efas.}{\insrsid4605116  In this way, Bali enjoyed the opulences of the heavenly planets.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.15.1, SB 8.15.2, SB 8.15.1-2
