 been born, He begged the King for three paces of land. Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja agreed to give this land in charity, since this was very insignificant, but \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya, who could understand that V\'e4manadeva was Vi\'f1\'eb
u, the friend of the demigods, forbade Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja to give this land. \'c7ukr\'e4c\'e4rya advised Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja to withdraw his promise. He explained that in subduing others, in joking, in responding to danger, in acting for the welfare of others, and so on, one could refuse to fulfill one's promise, and there would be no fault. By this philosophy, \'c7ukr\'e4c
\'e4rya tried to dissuade Bali Mah\'e4r\'e4ja from giving land to Lord V\'e4manadeva.
