Atheists generally follow the Bauddha philosophical conclusion that at death everything is finished. Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu, being an atheist, thought this way. Because Lord Vi\'f1\'eb
u was not visible to him, he thought that the Lord was dead. Even today, many people follow the philosophy that God is dead. But God is never dead. Even the living entity, who is part of God, never dies. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Na j\'e4
yate mriyate v\'e4 kad\'e4cit:}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  "For the soul there is never birth or death." This is the statement of }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 (2.20). Even the ordinary living entity never takes birth or dies. What then is to be said of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, who is the chief of all living entities? He certainly never takes birth or dies. }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
Ajo'pi sann avyay\'e4tm\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  (Bg. 4.6). Both the Lord and the living entity exist as unborn and inexhaustible personalities. Thus Hira\'ebyaka\'e7ipu's conclusion that Vi\'f1\'ebu was dead was wrong.
\par }\pard\plain \s908\qj \fi340\li0\ri0\sa86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f35\fs24\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 As indicated by the words }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 yato n\'e4vartate pum\'e4n,}{
\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  there is certainly a spiritual kingdom, and if the living entity goes there, he never returns to this material world. This is also confirmed in }{\cs2542\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
 (4.9): }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 tyaktv\'e4 deha\'e0 punar janma naiti m\'e4m eti so'rjuna.}{\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116  Materially speaking, every living entity dies; death is inevitable. But those who are }{\cs1998\i\fs28\cf1\insrsid4605116 
