This chapter describes the Supreme Personality of Godhead's incarnation as a fish, and it also describes the saving of Mah\'e4r\'e4ja Satyavrata from an inundation.
\par }\pard \qj \fi360\li0\ri0\sb86\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\insrsid4605116 The Supreme Personality of Godhead expands Himself by }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 sv\'e4\'e0\'e7a}{\insrsid4605116  (His personal expansions) and }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 
vibhinn\'e4\'e0\'e7a}{\insrsid4605116  (His expansions as the living entities). As stated in }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4 }{\cs1998\insrsid4605116\charrsid6902816 (4.8)}{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 , paritr\'e4\'eb\'e4ya s\'e4dh\'fcn\'e4
\'e0 vin\'e4\'e7\'e4ya ca du\'f1k\'e5t\'e4m:}{\insrsid4605116  the Supreme Personality of Godhead appears on this planet for the protection of the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 s\'e4dhus,}{\insrsid4605116 
 or devotees, and for the destruction of the miscreants, or nondevotees. He especially descends to give protection to the cows, the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 br\'e4hma\'ebas,}{\insrsid4605116 
 the demigods, the devotees and the Vedic system of religion. Thus He appears in various forms}{\insrsid6902816 \emdash }{\insrsid4605116 sometimes as a fish, sometimes a boar, sometimes }{\insrsid12929836 N\'e5si\'e0hadeva}{\insrsid4605116 , sometimes V
\'e4manadeva and so on}{\insrsid6902816 \emdash }{\insrsid4605116 but in any form or incarnation, although He comes within the atmosphere of the material modes of nature, He is unaffected. This is a sign of His supreme controllin
g power. Although He comes within the material atmosphere, }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 m\'e4y\'e4}{\insrsid4605116  cannot touch Him. Therefore, no material qualities can be attributed to Him in any degree.
\par Once, at the end of the previous }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 kalpa,}{\insrsid4605116  a demon named Hayagr\'e9va wanted to take the Vedic knowledge away from Lord Brahm\'e4
 at the time of annihilation. Therefore the Supreme Personality of Godhead took the incarnation of a fish at the beginning of the period of Sv\'e4yambhuva Manu and saved the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Vedas.}{\insrsid4605116  During the reign of C\'e4k
\'f1u\'f1a Manu there was a king named Satyavrata, who was a great pious ruler. To save him, the Lord appeared as the fish incarnation for a second time. King Satyavrata later became the son of the sun-god and was known as \'c7r\'e4
ddhadeva. He was established as Manu by the Supreme Personality of Godhead.
\par To receive the favor of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, King Satyavrata engaged in the austerity of subsisting only by drinking water. Once, while performing this austerity on the bank of the K\'e5tam\'e4l\'e4
 River and offering oblations of water with the 
palm of his hand, he found a small fish. The fish appealed to the King for protection, asking the King to keep Him in a safe place. Although the King did not know that the small fish was the Supreme Personality of Godhead Himself, as a king he gave shelte
r
 to the fish and kept Him in a water jug. The fish, being the Supreme Personality of Godhead, wanted to show His potency to King Satyavrata, and thus He immediately expanded His body in such a way that He could no longer be kept in the jug of water. The K
i
ng then put the fish in a big well, but the well was also too small. Then the King put the fish in a lake, but the lake was also unsuitable. Finally the King put the fish in the sea, but even the sea could not accommodate Him. Thus the King understood tha
t
 the fish was no one else but the Supreme Personality of Godhead, and he requested the Lord to describe His incarnation as a fish. The Personality of Godhead, being pleased with the King, informed him that within a week there would be an inundation throug
hout the universe and that the fish incarnation would protect the King, along with the }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 \'e5\'f1is,}{\insrsid4605116 
 herbs, seeds and other living entities, in a boat, which would be attached to the fish's horn. After saying this, the Lord disappeared. King Satyavrata offered resp
ectful obeisances to the Supreme Lord and continued to meditate upon Him. In due course of time, annihilation took place, and the King saw a boat coming near. After getting aboard with learned }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 br\'e4hma\'ebas}{\insrsid4605116 
 and saintly persons, he offered prayers to worship the Supreme Personality of Godhead. The Supreme Lord is situated in everyone's heart, and thus he taught Mah\'e4r\'e4
ja Satyavrata and the saintly persons about Vedic knowledge from the core of the heart. King Satyavrata took his next birth as Vaivasvata Manu, who is mentioned in }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4.}{\insrsid4605116  }{
\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Vivasv\'e4n manave pr\'e4ha:}{\insrsid4605116  the sun-god spoke the science of }{\cs1998\i\insrsid4605116 Bhagavad-g\'e9t\'e4}{\insrsid4605116  to his son Manu. Because of being the son of Vivasv\'e4
n, this Manu is known as Vaivasvata Manu.
\par }\pard\plain \s1467\qr \li0\ri0\sb240\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \v\f35\fs20\cf11\lang2057\langfe2057\cgrid\langnp2057\langfenp2057 {\insrsid4605116 SB 8.24.1
